\title{Algebraic and transcendental numbers}
\taxon{Remark}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Classically #{\alpha \in \mathbb{C}} is called algebraic/transcendental if it is algebraic/transcendental over #{\mathbb{Q}}.}

\p{A countability argument (see IA Numbers and Sets) shows that transcendental numbers exist. Liouville showed that #{\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{10^{n!}}} is transcendental.}

\p{It was proved in the 19th century that #{e} and #{\pi} are transcendental (Hermite and Lindemann).}
